    {
      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To determine the probability that it will rain on both Saturday and Sunday, we start by noting that the probability of rain on each day is independent of the other. This means that the probability of rain on both days is the product of the probabilities of rain on each individual day.\n\nThe probability that it will rain on Saturday is given as \\(60\\%\\), which can be written as \\(0.60\\). The probability that it will rain on Sunday is given as \\(25\\%\\), which can be written as \\(0.25\\).\n\nSince the events are independent, the probability that it will rain on both Saturday and Sunday is:\n\\[\n0.60 \\times 0.25\n\\]\n\nNext, we calculate the product:\n\\[\n0.60 \\times 0.25 = 0.15\n\\]\n\nTo express this probability as a percent, we multiply by \\(100\\):\n\\[\n0.15 \\times 100 = 15\\%\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the probability that it will rain on both Saturday and Sunday is \\(\\boxed{15}\\).",
