    {
      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "We are given that the probability of rain on Saturday is \\(60\\%\\) and the probability of rain on Sunday is \\(25\\%\\). Since the probabilities are given as percentages, we can convert them to decimal form for easier calculations:\n\\[\n\\text{Probability of rain on Saturday} = 0.60\n\\]\n\\[\n\\text{Probability of rain on Sunday} = 0.25\n\\]\n\nTo find the probability that it will rain on both Saturday and Sunday, we use the fact that the events are independent. The probability of both independent events occurring is the product of their individual probabilities:\n\\[\n\\text{Probability of rain on both days} = \\left( \\text{Probability of rain on Saturday} \\right) \\times \\left( \\text{Probability of rain on Sunday} \\right)\n\\]\nSubstituting the values we have:\n\\[\n\\text{Probability of rain on both days} = 0.60 \\times 0.25\n\\]\n\nNext, we perform the multiplication:\n\\[\n0.60 \\times 0.25 = 0.15\n\\]\n\nTo express this probability as a percent, we convert the decimal back to a percentage by multiplying by \\(100\\):\n\\[\n0.15 \\times 100 = 15\\%\n\\]\n\nThus, the probability that it will rain on both days is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{15}\n\\]",
